#
# `-f` on the command line — #2315's zsh/ half, and the letter a portable
# script is most likely to reach for without checking.
#
# In bash, ksh93 and dash, `-f` is noglob: `sh -f -c 'printf "[%s]\n" *'`
# prints the pattern. In zsh the letter is `NO_RCS` — "read no startup files"
# — and globbing is untouched, so the same command line prints the file names.
# zsh spells noglob `-F`.
#
# zsh/options.tests records the same split for `set -f` *inside* a running
# shell. This file is the other route, and the route is the point: `zsh -f` is
# how a script asks for a shell with no startup files, and it is written into
# shebangs and into `#!/usr/bin/env` lines where the writer usually means one
# of the two things and gets the other.
#
# `-o noglob`, which every column shares, is what core/invocation.tests uses
# and is asked here too — beside the letter, so the file says the option
# exists and the *letter* is what moved.
#
# `$SUITE_SHELL` is the shell of this run; see suite.OurShellVar.

: > aaa.txt
: > bbb.txt

# The letter, three ways of writing it, and the option's long name beside it.
"$SUITE_SHELL" -f -c 'printf "dash f: [%s]\n" *.txt'
"$SUITE_SHELL" -fc 'printf "bundled: [%s]\n" *.txt'
"$SUITE_SHELL" -o noglob -c 'printf "the option name: [%s]\n" *.txt'

# And with no option at all, so the three lines above are read against
# something rather than against an assumption.
"$SUITE_SHELL" -c 'printf "no option: [%s]\n" *.txt'

# `$0` read inside a function of a `-c` command string is the *function's*
# name here and the shell's `$0` in the other three — the same rule
# zsh/functions.tests records for a function called from a script, seen on the
# route where a script is one string on the command line.
"$SUITE_SHELL" -c 'f() { printf "in a function [%s]\n" "$0"; }
f' givenname
"$SUITE_SHELL" -c 'printf "outside a function [%s]\n" "$0"' givenname
